style \\int_{0}^{2 cos ⁡ θ}\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{2}r 3dzdrdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126618} {:answer/id #uuid "0ca051cd-833c-5347-aa63-bcbbf6080651" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π / 2}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{-2 sin ⁡ θ}\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{2}r 3dzdrdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126619}] :answer-field/id #uuid "5de2e9ed-6a8e-5e65-b0f4-c004ab1d9bd0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126614}] :question/id #uuid "cc7985de-f7b8-5b62-b788-d731ea9f3ce6" :question/math-academy-id "q-161523" :question/problem "If the solid region $R$ is given by\n$R = {(x, y, z): (x - 1)^{2} + y^{2} \\le 1, y \\ge 0, 1 \\le z \\le 2}$,\nthen $∭_(R)x^{2} + y^{2}dV =$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367722 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b265b7cc-feb9-5f37-9252-3a78cad0bd5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{-8}^{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+1}}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367724} {:answer/id #uuid "8580f07f-10cc-5678-a359-0d89f29a5582" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{-8}^{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+1}}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367725} {:answer/id #uuid "5d8af12c-fb84-5a5a-a7a2-521fb178be84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{-8}^{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+1}}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367726} {:answer/id #uuid "54950957-60fb-56ff-96f5-e10b21a3d9ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{-8}^{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+1}}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367727} {:answer/id #uuid "dff4f68f-af71-5932-8072-ae940cffd5e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{-8}^{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+1}}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367728}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dff4f68f-af71-5932-8072-ae940cffd5e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{-8}^{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+1}}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367728} :answer-field/id #uuid "8751e409-d7fb-5aec-8818-db14c330ee72" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367723}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f13fe1ec-6a87-5577-82ff-9825a15656c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-161630" :question/problem "If the solid region $R$ is given by \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x+3)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 9,\\,-8\\le z\\le x+2},\n$$\n\nthen ${∭}_{R}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}V=$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/3c/3c03dde21942c60de527ace896bb51ad8070781126d72f5be5e2f6069700736d.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $3$ whose axis passes through the point $(-3,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=-8,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=x+2=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's write the equation $(x+3)^{2}+{y}^{2}=9$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =9 \\\\ {(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3)}^{2}+{(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2} & =9 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +6r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +9+{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =9 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+6r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}+6r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le -6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2},\\,-8\\le z\\le r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+1}}\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+1}}\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{-8}^{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+1}}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367729 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19b100d2-4636-55d3-9270-cc48d2b6fc52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }^{6}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{z+1}}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367731} {:answer/id #uuid "9a29a4a1-8401-5c26-985a-c9e53ed3d39d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }^{6}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{z+1}}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367732} {:answer/id #uuid "cfe69c16-aea5-5c84-b3e2-6e91f9235a94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }^{6}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{z+1}}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367733} {:answer/id #uuid "6d67683a-c100-5cd2-85f2-e42c45195bdc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }^{6}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{z+1}}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367734} {:answer/id #uuid "30a2338f-aa3a-5e0e-a5d0-96cd484338d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }^{6}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{z+1}}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367735}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19b100d2-4636-55d3-9270-cc48d2b6fc52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }^{6}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{z+1}}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367731} :answer-field/id #uuid "29402b40-ddfd-5d71-9239-ec1aa69d5ac0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367730}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d873cc92-450d-547b-9f0c-590b7060a8cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-161636" :question/problem "If the solid region $R$ is given by \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x-3)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 9,\\,y\\le 0,\\,y\\le z\\le 6},\n$$\n\nthen ${∭}_{R}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{z+1}}\\,\\text{d}V=$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/cc/ccc5cab37aed89c0d701ce0195414796211ddc73a498eaaa1ff145a45c42e9fd.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $3$ whose axis passes through the point $(3,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=6.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $(x-3)^{2}+{y}^{2}=9$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =9 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} & =3 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -6r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +9+{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =9 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-6r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-6r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le z\\le 6}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{z+1}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{z+1}}\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }^{6}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{z+1}}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427905 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07316687-6f83-5fed-b3ec-6bddf779b76b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{-2}^{\\sqrt{16-{r}^{2}}}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427865} {:answer/id #uuid "8a070aa5-47af-5201-85a2-9449ca643060" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{-2}^{\\sqrt{16-{r}^{2}}}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427880} {:answer/id #uuid "aa6ce7d9-8ba5-51ed-8961-057d4fc31ae0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{-2}^{\\sqrt{16-{r}^{2}}}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427884} {:answer/id #uuid "b00512c6-580b-5ace-be32-2aca9c459007" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{-2}^{\\sqrt{16-{r}^{2}}}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427885} {:answer/id #uuid "c7c92edf-23c0-5c0c-b7bb-0f570aaf2732" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{-2}^{\\sqrt{16-{r}^{2}}}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427887}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a070aa5-47af-5201-85a2-9449ca643060" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{-2}^{\\sqrt{16-{r}^{2}}}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427880} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a4c28d8-7673-5151-abc1-64dbe75c6bbb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427898}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f86783a2-30c3-5d18-9eb0-dc2efdbbae6a" :question/math-academy-id "q-161633" :question/problem "If the solid region $R$ is given by \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+(y-2)^{2}\\le 4,\\,x\\le 0,\\,-2\\le z\\le \\sqrt{16-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}\\},\n$$\n\nthen ${∭}_{R}2xy\\,\\text{d}V=$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/83/831de8cf1d9833f8674a7e4cb69d01098970eefb9cf576c0537fa7be471c8c2c.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $2$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,2)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=-2,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=\\sqrt{16-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}=\\sqrt{16-{r}^{2}}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=4$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4 & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,-2\\le z\\le \\sqrt{16-{r}^{2}}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}2xy\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}2(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{-2}^{\\sqrt{16-{r}^{2}}}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427906 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6caf10ce-b939-51d0-8ac6-b9727918700b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{-5}^{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+1}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427874} {:answer/id #uuid "88813988-bed1-58d5-9361-f7b9c3b921af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{-5}^{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }r\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+1}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427879} {:answer/id #uuid "aa5a0734-d3ec-5220-8c59-ae2d980b4839" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{-5}^{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }r\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+1}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427883} {:answer/id #uuid "d4e25a86-fc0b-500c-9ea6-617a19745775" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{-5}^{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+1}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427888} {:answer/id #uuid "f1e27cb0-5d40-54cb-b1f4-f82c347f9fc7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{-5}^{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }r\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+1}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427891}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "88813988-bed1-58d5-9361-f7b9c3b921af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{-5}^{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }r\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+1}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427879} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae592001-7c80-5683-9d64-6ebfa88e4b3c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427899}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fa2c3789-4a9d-5deb-a378-eba9cd152cf2" :question/math-academy-id "q-161637" :question/problem "If the solid region $R$ is given by \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+(y-4)^{2}\\le 16,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,-5\\le z\\le x\\},\n$$\n\nthen ${∭}_{R}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}V=$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/b6/b66b8db18b2f5773addc2d00c02f2f15f4a7ead53323418e63bd50457f74466b.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $4$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,4)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=-5,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+(y-4)^{2}=16$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y-4)^{2} & =16 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -4)^{2} & =16 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -8r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +16 & =16 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-8r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-8r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,-5\\le z\\le r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+1}\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+1}\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{-5}^{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }r\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+1}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536983 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "237e8e7d-e3db-5f7c-9347-4abfc31811d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{0}^{1}r^{3}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536996} {:answer/id #uuid "4f83f8a6-2631-55b9-a475-cf21a17cb881" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{1}^{0}r^{3}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537015} {:answer/id #uuid "9d691199-900d-5dbc-95ff-fd5da1703bb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{0}^{1}r^{4}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537043} {:answer/id #uuid "b727e49c-dd3f-5d45-b030-cf65baa91799" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{0}^{1}r^{2}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537053} {:answer/id #uuid "f8d59057-b6e1-58d4-8bca-71b7a0d1be8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{2 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{0}^{1}r^{3}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537076}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "237e8e7d-e3db-5f7c-9347-4abfc31811d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{0}^{1}r^{3}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536996} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb63a029-3502-5e90-a9fa-8ddfb79c7c2d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537078}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "00623118-ee32-53b0-8e64-30db43b56fa6" :question/problem "If the solid region $R$ is given by \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4,y\\ge 0,\\,0\\le z\\le 1\\},\n$$\n\nthen express the triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}x^{2} + y^{2}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$\n\nas an equivalent integral in cylindrical polar coordinates.\n\n{{selection}}" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/d2/d2b3344b7b910f5685d4fd81b75a600608af6b008778ff608cb084edb5a92da9.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $2$ whose axis passes through the point $(2,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta-2)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta)^{2} & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta+4+{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta & =0 \\\\ r(r-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)},\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi}{2},\\,0\\le z\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}x^{2} + y^{2}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}((r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta)^{2})\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{0}^{1}r^{3}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537003 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30aa1de6-9825-58df-a0bb-9361d44a7568" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{- 4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{0}^{r^{2} + 5}r^{3} \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536998} {:answer/id #uuid "32b99b29-cd14-5ea5-9fc1-15df5b4eb850" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{- 4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{0}^{r^{2} + 5}r^{3} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537001} {:answer/id #uuid "9605e9f5-cd80-5de4-9598-0eef1d67fdca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{- 4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{0}^{r^{2} + 5}r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537039} {:answer/id #uuid "9c51e829-74a8-59f0-b024-a19f558413ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{- 4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{0}^{r^{2} + 5}r^{2} \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537042} {:answer/id #uuid "dff20a83-a22c-52be-b879-f991bfe4e845" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{- 4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{0}^{r^{2} + 5}r^{3} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537068}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "32b99b29-cd14-5ea5-9fc1-15df5b4eb850" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{- 4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{0}^{r^{2} + 5}r^{3} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537001} :answer-field/id #uuid "c677a795-89fc-56d8-8a2d-f295aa205e01" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537056}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3421c850-1e07-524f-a0e9-45c83fcec039" :question/problem "If the solid region $R$ is given by \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2}\\le 4,\\,0\\le z\\le x^{2} + y^{2} + 5\\},\n$$\n\nthen express the triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}x y\\,\\text{d}V\n$$\n\nas an equivalent integral in cylindrical polar coordinates.\n\n{{selection}}" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/ce/cee08173667c9577f4c7aab8cac5386434f2e1ecab8836150ebe8066d1cbaa68.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $2$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,-2)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=x^{2} + y^{2} + 5=r^{2} + 5.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's write the equation ${x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2}=4$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta+2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta+4 & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}+4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta & =0 \\\\ r(r+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=- 4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le - 4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)},\\,\\pi\\le \\theta \\le 2 \\pi,\\,0\\le z\\le r^{2} + 5\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}x y\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta)(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta)\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{- 4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{0}^{r^{2} + 5}r^{3} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537006 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "321df476-4949-5c09-820e-9305d838bc49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{8 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{-5}^{\\sqrt{81 - r^{2}}}2 r^{3} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537000} {:answer/id #uuid "4e59d9d4-da68-5e58-aff1-c4c4a3337e4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{8 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{-5}^{\\sqrt{81 - r^{2}}}2 r^{4} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537014} {:answer/id #uuid "9a68c5dc-993d-52d2-9754-5b09d9106e51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{-5}^{\\sqrt{81 - r^{2}}}2 r^{3} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537041} {:answer/id #uuid "b4961f55-574a-53f6-91ac-244a2907cf1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{8 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{81 - r^{2}}}^{-5}2 r^{3} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537051} {:answer/id #uuid "c82cc82c-e96d-5c71-9bfd-49718a58969e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{8 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{-5}^{\\sqrt{81 - r^{2}}}2 r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537059}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "321df476-4949-5c09-820e-9305d838bc49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{8 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{-5}^{\\sqrt{81 - r^{2}}}2 r^{3} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537000} :answer-field/id #uuid "346ca32c-f65c-5df0-9d38-5355dcbfffd3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537004}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3595f3ec-49d1-5481-82e6-c23464cfa603" :question/problem "If the solid region $R$ is given by \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+(y-4)^{2}\\le 16,x\\le 0,\\,-5\\le z\\le \\sqrt{- x^{2} - y^{2} + 81}\\},\n$$\n\nthen express the triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}2 x y\\,\\text{d}V\n$$\n\nas an equivalent integral in cylindrical polar coordinates.\n\n{{selection}}" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/57/5764f328a614ddaa96abd28a4568c4cd7dfcf0988033d3122bdce0e1e52b7bc5.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $4$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,4)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=-5,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=\\sqrt{- x^{2} - y^{2} + 81}=\\sqrt{81 - r^{2}}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+(y-4)^{2}=16$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y-4)^{2} & =16 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta-4)^{2} & =16 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -8r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta+16 & =16 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-8r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-8r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta & =0 \\\\ r(r-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=8 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 8 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)},\\,\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi,\\,-5\\le z\\le \\sqrt{81 - r^{2}}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}2 x y\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}2(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta)(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta)\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{8 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{-5}^{\\sqrt{81 - r^{2}}}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537019 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30efa546-da45-5a01-977f-f55cd5e7ba61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{4}^{7}r^{3}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536999} {:answer/id #uuid "3dc4f541-1cf6-594c-ba21-8359f5bbbc24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{7}^{4}r^{3}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537011} {:answer/id #uuid "7a324aba-9b63-5874-a2b1-733a50c7781b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{4}^{7}r^{4}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537033} {:answer/id #uuid "81de05b4-209e-5bc0-9833-10807223dd7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{2 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{4}^{7}r^{3}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537035} {:answer/id #uuid "e388a7a5-3cdc-52bc-8ddf-defa6c22aae8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{4}^{7}r^{2}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537070}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "30efa546-da45-5a01-977f-f55cd5e7ba61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{4}^{7}r^{3}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536999} :answer-field/id #uuid "65f21aac-e7ac-5168-a171-1b5b4f594e1c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537022}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "590500ab-ca5a-598f-be1f-2910c46fae9a" :question/problem "If the solid region $R$ is given by \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4,y\\ge 0,\\,4\\le z\\le 7\\},\n$$\n\nthen express the triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}x^{2} + y^{2}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$\n\nas an equivalent integral in cylindrical polar coordinates.\n\n{{selection}}" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/d2/d2b3344b7b910f5685d4fd81b75a600608af6b008778ff608cb084edb5a92da9.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $2$ whose axis passes through the point $(2,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=4,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=7.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta-2)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta)^{2} & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta+4+{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta & =0 \\\\ r(r-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)},\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi}{2},\\,4\\le z\\le 7\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}x^{2} + y^{2}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}((r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta)^{2})\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{4}^{7}r^{3}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537025 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0da30d8b-b4d8-596a-8c80-6b14e0374096" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{8 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{4}^{7}r^{2}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536987} {:answer/id #uuid "0e208e2f-cf97-52a6-913e-893119de1c31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{8 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{4}^{7}r^{3}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536988} {:answer/id #uuid "20ec8899-9f98-5939-b1be-717c8a36b84e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{8 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{4}^{7}r^{4}\\,dz\\,\\tex